# Profile: phase2
# The durable write ledger, the authority store, scoped credentials and enforced route policy, all on at
# once, for the THROWAWAY gate stack only. Product defaults are never changed by this file.
#
# requires: compose
#
# GATE BOOTSTRAP (run these steps in this order; the project name and paths are the gate's own):
#  0. This profile runs on its OWN restart, after the flags-on union runs. It is NOT part of that union:
#     NS_POLICY_MODE=enforce denies every identity the authority store does not know, which would break the
#     other profiles' scenarios on the same stack.
#  1. The `default` profile must run BEFORE it, on the stack as it was, with NS_RUN_ID=<run id> exported.
#     Reason: the acceptance users are `acc-<run id>-a` and `acc-<run id>-b`; the migration in step 2 can
#     only bind users it can see in the stores, and it sees them from the memories they wrote. The default
#     run writes private and shared facts as both users, in the run's project, which is all it needs.
#     Never write data as `acc-<run id>-outsider` (it must stay unknown to the authority store).
#     Markers inside the scenarios carry their own per-run nonce, so a rerun with the same NS_RUN_ID is safe.
#  2. Bind those users and publish their grants, in the API container of the stack that just ran:
#       docker compose -p <gate project> exec -T <api service> python -m policy_migration apply \
#         --tenant acc --authority-sqlite /data/ingest/phase2-authority.db \
#         --qdrant-url http://<qdrant service>:6333 --collection <qdrant collection> \
#         --no-legacy-dictator-grants
#     (`<qdrant collection>` is the stack's collection, `neuralscape_memories` unless overridden.) Only pools
#     seen at this point are granted, plus the project-less pools; the phase2 scenarios use project-less pools.
#  3. Restart the API and ALL THREE workers (fast, graph, ingest) with every KEY=value line of this file added
#     to their environment. The two SQLite files below live on the API's `ingest_data` volume (/data/ingest).
#  4. Run: NS_RUN_ID=<same run id> NS_COMMIT=<sha> acceptance/run.sh <url> --compose-project <gate project> \
#       --profile phase2 --out <file>
#     The story restarts the API once (last fixture step) and waits for /health.
#  5. Second tenant (scenarios for tenant cells only; they stay red until that slice exists): export
#     NS_USER_T2=<user id of a member of a second tenant> and NS_TOKEN_T2=<its token>, minted like the other
#     acceptance tokens: docker compose -p <gate project> exec -T <api service> python scripts/issue_user_token.py
#     --user <that id> --days 1. The user must be bound into a different tenant first (tenant-cell
#     bootstrap); without both variables those scenarios fail with a message saying so.
#  6. Optional, for the member-journey scenarios: bind acceptance user A as the tenant's first administrator
#     (the membership slice's operator command) before step 4; without it they stay red.
# The ledger is a single-host SQLite file: when the write path starts using it, the worker that applies
# intents must mount the same volume.

LEDGER_ENABLED=true
LEDGER_BACKEND=sqlite
LEDGER_SQLITE_PATH=/data/ingest/phase2-ledger.db

AUTHORITY_ENABLED=true
AUTHORITY_BACKEND=sqlite
AUTHORITY_SQLITE_PATH=/data/ingest/phase2-authority.db

NS_POLICY_MODE=enforce
CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=true
